BCC as a 16-bit program: arguments that a prototype widens from int to long.

BCC's sources declare offset_T and value_t as long, and on the 32- and 64-bit
hosts BCC is normally built on, int and long are the same size, so passing an
int where a long is expected costs nothing. Built as a 16-bit XENIX program in
K&R mode, the callee reads a 32-bit argument from a 16-bit push and picks up
garbage in the high word: frame offsets came out as $40004 instead of 4.
These 15 call sites were found with gcc -Wtraditional-conversion; each gets an
explicit cast. No change in behaviour on hosts where int and long agree.

diff -ruN a/bcc/declare.c b/bcc/declare.c
--- a/bcc/declare.c
+++ b/bcc/declare.c
@@ -716,7 +716,7 @@
 	    outstr(funcname);
 	    outstr(".off = ");
 #ifndef I80386
-	    outnhex(4);
+	    outnhex((uoffset_T) 4);
 #else
 	    outnhex(i386_32?12:4);
 #endif
